1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,494
A company built an assistant to answer employee questions about benefits.

2
00:00:04,494 --> 00:00:08,751
Version one was a system prompt describing the company and a capable model.

3
00:00:08,751 --> 00:00:10,681
It worked beautifully in the demo.

4
00:00:10,681 --> 00:00:15,279
Then someone asked it a real question: how many vacation days do I get after five

5
00:00:15,279 --> 00:00:15,620
years.

6
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:20,161
The model answered instantly and confidently, a tidy, plausible tiered schedule.

7
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:24,816
It was completely wrong for this company, and a manager approved time off based on

8
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:26,860
that answer before anyone caught it.

9
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:27,825
That's the story.

10
00:00:27,825 --> 00:00:30,833
Everything about why RAG exists is sitting inside it.

11
00:00:30,833 --> 00:00:35,318
Notice what makes this failure hard to catch in review, not just in production.

12
00:00:35,318 --> 00:00:38,610
The wrong answer read exactly like a right one would have.

13
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:42,981
Confident tone, plausible structure, nothing about the phrasing gave it away.

14
00:00:42,981 --> 00:00:47,750
That's the hallucination mechanism from Section one, and it's worth sitting with for

15
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:52,518
a moment before moving to the fix, because the fix only makes sense once you've felt

16
00:00:52,518 --> 00:00:55,186
how invisible the failure was from the outside.

17
00:00:55,736 --> 00:01:00,246
Look closely at what actually changed between those two columns, because it's less

18
00:01:00,246 --> 00:01:01,236
than you'd expect.

19
00:01:01,236 --> 00:01:02,941
The model didn't get retrained.

20
00:01:02,941 --> 00:01:07,507
It didn't get more capable at reasoning, or better at maths, or more careful in any

21
00:01:07,507 --> 00:01:08,277
general sense.

22
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:12,732
What changed is that the second version had the actual policy document sitting in

23
00:01:12,732 --> 00:01:17,352
its context when it answered, and the first version was reasoning entirely from what

24
00:01:17,352 --> 00:01:21,973
vacation policies generally look like, the mechanism from lecture one, doing exactly

25
00:01:21,973 --> 00:01:24,008
what it does when nothing grounds it.

26
00:01:24,008 --> 00:01:27,308
This is also why the fix couldn't have been a better prompt.

27
00:01:27,308 --> 00:01:31,928
No amount of instructing the model to "be accurate about vacation policy" gives it a

28
00:01:31,928 --> 00:01:36,494
number that was never in its training data and never will be, because it's specific

29
00:01:36,494 --> 00:01:38,364
to one company's private document.

30
00:01:38,364 --> 00:01:40,674
Some information isn't a phrasing problem.

31
00:01:40,674 --> 00:01:45,294
It's an access problem, and only one fix on the whole decision tree from Section two

32
00:01:45,294 --> 00:01:47,329
actually solves that particular kind.

33
00:01:47,879 --> 00:01:52,178
Here's the same story again, on the stack diagram from earlier in this course, and

34
00:01:52,178 --> 00:01:53,698
it makes the point precisely.

35
00:01:53,698 --> 00:01:55,742
Four of five layers didn't move at all.

36
00:01:55,742 --> 00:01:59,830
Same product, same guardrails, same orchestration code, same underlying model.

37
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:04,181
Exactly one layer changed: the data layer went from empty to populated with the one

38
00:02:04,181 --> 00:02:05,858
document that actually mattered.

39
00:02:05,858 --> 00:02:08,165
RAG isn't a smarter version of your feature.

40
00:02:08,165 --> 00:02:12,148
It's the same feature, finally given the thing it needed to answer honestly.

41
00:02:12,148 --> 00:02:16,552
Keep this table in your back pocket for the rest of this section, because it answers

42
00:02:16,552 --> 00:02:20,745
a question that comes up constantly once you start designing one of these: which

43
00:02:20,745 --> 00:02:23,471
team actually owns fixing it when something's wrong.

44
00:02:23,471 --> 00:02:27,874
If the fact was missing, that's a data-layer problem, and it belongs to whoever owns

45
00:02:27,874 --> 00:02:30,652
the document, not to engineering re-tuning the model.

46
00:02:30,652 --> 00:02:34,950
Naming the layer, the exact skill from Section two, is what turns a vague "the bot

47
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:37,047
is wrong" into someone's actual Tuesday.

48
00:02:37,597 --> 00:02:41,797
Say the honest caveat, because RAG gets reached for as a universal fix, and it

49
00:02:41,797 --> 00:02:43,682
solves exactly one kind of failure.

50
00:02:43,682 --> 00:02:48,044
The vacation policy question was a missing-fact failure: the answer existed, in a

51
00:02:48,044 --> 00:02:50,521
document, and the model simply didn't have it.

52
00:02:50,521 --> 00:02:52,244
Retrieval fixes that completely.

53
00:02:52,244 --> 00:02:56,444
It does nothing for a feature that's misinterpreting a chart, doing arithmetic

54
00:02:56,444 --> 00:03:00,591
wrong, or drawing a bad conclusion from information it already had correctly.

55
00:03:00,591 --> 00:03:04,737
Handing that kind of failure more documents doesn't touch the actual problem.

56
00:03:04,737 --> 00:03:09,153
Watch for this specifically when a team proposes "adding RAG" as a fix for a vague

57
00:03:09,153 --> 00:03:13,461
complaint like "it's not being smart enough." That phrase almost never tells you

58
00:03:13,461 --> 00:03:15,184
which failure you're looking at.

59
00:03:15,184 --> 00:03:19,546
Ask for one concrete wrong answer first, then diagnose it, before committing to a

60
00:03:19,546 --> 00:03:22,561
retrieval system that might not even be the right layer.

61
00:03:23,111 --> 00:03:27,887
That's the test worth carrying into every "should we add RAG here" conversation from

62
00:03:27,887 --> 00:03:31,127
now on: was the fact missing, or was the reasoning wrong.

63
00:03:31,127 --> 00:03:34,652
Run the exercise on a real wrong answer from your own feature.

64
00:03:34,652 --> 00:03:39,313
If the honest diagnosis is a reasoning failure, adding retrieval will make the PRD

65
00:03:39,313 --> 00:03:42,951
look more sophisticated and change nothing about the actual bug.

66
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:45,112
One thing before you move on.

67
00:03:45,112 --> 00:03:47,500
Pull up a real case your feature got wrong.

68
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,109
Was the fact missing, or was the reasoning bad?

69
00:03:50,109 --> 00:03:54,663
Run it through this lecture's test honestly, rather than assuming you already know

70
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:55,274
the answer.

71
00:03:55,274 --> 00:03:59,772
If access was the culprit, push one step further: if you can't point to where the

72
00:03:59,772 --> 00:04:04,325
right answer actually lives, that's worth knowing too, because no retrieval system

73
00:04:04,325 --> 00:04:06,991
can find a document that was never written down.

74
00:04:06,991 --> 00:04:11,489
Next lecture opens the pipeline properly: every stage between a question arriving

75
00:04:11,489 --> 00:04:15,431
and an answer coming back, and exactly which stages are yours to shape.
